\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The maximizers of the hopping weight}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{On a nonempty finite connected graph, give the real scalar module a
positive definite invariant inner product and take an orbit
#{\mathcal O} of an isometric group action. Give each edge a positive
real weight #{w_e}. The \vocabk{hopping weight}{fdip-00ZW} is
##{\xi_{\rm hop}(\phi)=
 \prod_{e\in E}\ue^{w_e\langle\phi_{h(e)},\phi_{s(e)}\rangle}.}
For any constant configuration #{\phi^{\rm c}} in this orbit,
##{\frac{\xi_{\rm hop}(\phi^{\rm c})}{\xi_{\rm hop}(\phi)}
 =\ue^{\frac12\sum_{e\in E}w_e\|\phi_{h(e)}-\phi_{s(e)}\|^2}.}
Exactly the constant configurations maximize this weight. For a
finite orbit there are #{|\mathcal O|} maximizers, permuted
transitively by the global group action.}

\proof{
\p{All orbit values have the same squared norm #{\|\phi^\circ\|^2}.
For two such values #{u,v}, expansion of the squared difference
gives #{\|\phi^\circ\|^2-\langle u,v\rangle=\|u-v\|^2/2}.
Multiplying the edge ratios proves the formula. Each summand is
nonnegative, and positive definiteness and #{w_e>0} force equal
endpoint values whenever the sum vanishes. Equality propagates along
paths in the underlying connected graph. Conversely each constant
configuration attains the displayed numerator. The choice of its
single value identifies the set of maximizers with the orbit.}
}
